For a Boolean polynomial or function $f$, its Hamming weight $\lvert f\rvert$ is the number of inputs at which
$f$ equals $1$, equivalently the Hamming weight of its evaluation vector. Hence
the same notation applies when $f$ represents an evaluation vector in a
Reed--Muller code or coset. In particular,
$\lvert \chi_M\rvert=\lvert X\rvert$, which equals the number of columns because the columns are
distinct.
Note every Boolean function $g$ on $\mathbb{F}_2^r$ has a unique multilinear expansion
\[
g=\sum_{T\subseteq\{1,\dots,r\}}c_T\prod_{l\in T}v_l,
\qquad c_T\in\mathbb{F}_2 ,
\]
and its degree is the largest
$\lvert T\rvert$ with $c_T=1$.  If $\chi_M$ has degree $\deg\chi_M$, then it belongs to the Reed--Muller code $\operatorname{RM}(\deg\chi_M,\,r)$.

\label{def:exp-CI}
$G=[K;S]$ admits a \emph{$T/T^\dagger$ implementation} of CCZ if there is $\Gamma\in\{\pm1\}^n$ with
\begin{equation}\label{eq:exp-CI}
\mathrm{sw}_\Gamma\!\bigl(K^{\!\top}\vec x+S^{\!\top}\vec y\bigr)\equiv 4\,x_1x_2x_3\pmod 8
\qquad\forall\,\vec x\in\mathbb{F}_2^3,\ \vec y\in\mathbb{F}_2^s,
\end{equation}
