This proof covers the necessity direction~(ii).
Assume that $C\,T^{\otimes n}$ realizes logical CCZ and set
$\vec z=(\vec x,\vec y)$, $\vec f=G^{\!\top}\vec z$, and $\omega=e^{\pi i/4}$. Every diagonal
Clifford is, up to a global phase, expressed as a product of $Z$, $S$, and $CZ$ gates, so $C$
may be taken in that form. Physical $T$ gates contribute $\omega^{\lvert \vec f\rvert}$, while $C$
contributes $\omega^{2h(\vec f)}$. Equating the combined phase
$\omega^{\lvert \vec f\rvert+2h(\vec f)}$ to $\omega^{4x_1x_2x_3}$ gives
\begin{equation*}
\lvert \vec f\rvert+2h(\vec f)\equiv4x_1x_2x_3\pmod8,
\qquad
h(\vec f)=\sum_j\lambda_j f_j+\sum_{j<j'}\lambda_{jj'}f_jf_{j'},
\end{equation*}
where $\vec f=K^{\!\top}\vec x+S^{\!\top}\vec y$,
$\lambda_j\in\{0,1,2,3\}$, and $\lambda_{jj'}\in\{0,2\}$, as in
\eqref{eq:exp-FQT-CCZ} and~\eqref{eq:exp-correction}.
Logical CCZ up to a global phase would add a constant $c$ to the right-hand side.
The constant changes only the constant term of the expansion in $\vec z$, which the conditions below do
not use. At $\vec z=0$ the left-hand side is $0$, so $c\equiv0\pmod8$ in any case.

List the rows of $G$ as $g^{\,1},\ldots,g^{\,3+s}$, with $g^{\,i}=K_i$ for
$1\le i\le3$ and $g^{\,3+l}=S_l$ for $1\le l\le s$. For a nonempty index set
